# One verifier, run by someone who is not the deployment's operator.
#
#   cp .env.verifier-standalone.example .env
#   docker compose -f infra/docker-compose.verifier.yml up --build
#
# This is the whole configuration. There is no database URL, no API port, no
# admin or keeper key, and no deploy block: a verifier reads the contract, 0G
# Storage and 0G Compute directly and never talks to the operator's services.
# If you find yourself needing a value that is not here, that is a bug in the
# verifier, not in your setup — please report it.

# ─── Chain ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Everything network-shaped — RPC, storage indexer, compute router and model —
# is defaulted from CHAIN_ID. Override a URL only to point at your own node.
CHAIN_ID=16661
OG_RPC_URL=https://evmrpc.0g.ai
PROOFRELAY_ADDRESS=0xD3101C19175b50fD47C9e0B14A2dc63485f527D1

# ─── Your verifier ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The key that registers, commits, reveals and collects. It is your onchain
# identity in this market; fund it with 0G (see docs/VERIFIER_OPERATOR.md for
# how much) and keep it to yourself.
#
# These two lines are coupled. VERIFIER_PROFILE is spliced, uppercased, into
# the name of the key variable and of every other per-verifier setting below —
# profile `a` reads VERIFIER_A_PRIVATE_KEY, profile `nodesproof` would read
# VERIFIER_NODESPROOF_PRIVATE_KEY. Rename one without the other and the worker
# stops at "VERIFIER_<NAME>_PRIVATE_KEY is not set".
VERIFIER_PROFILE=a
VERIFIER_A_PRIVATE_KEY=
VERIFIER_POLL_MS=3000

# ─── Make your pipeline yours ────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# CHANGE COMPUTE_MODEL. It is the only setting in this file that can change a
# verdict, and leaving it is the one mistake this file makes easy.
#
# The profile letter picks a preset, and `a` is what this example ships:
#   a = depth 2, threshold 0.55      c = depth 3, threshold 0.60
#   b = depth 3, threshold 0.50      d = depth 2, threshold 0.48
# Any other letter falls back to depth 2, threshold 0.55 — the same as `a`.
#
# The letter also names you: the worker writes `verifier-<letter>` into the
# metadata it registers with, so a copy of this file registers onchain as
# "verifier-a" — a name the market already has. You may use a word instead of
# a letter, and `VERIFIER_PROFILE=nodesproof` registers as "verifier-nodesproof".
# If you do, rename the settings with it — the private key above and the two
# lines at the end of this block all carry the profile name — and rename them
# BEFORE you register, because metadata is written once, at registration
# (`ensureRegistered` only writes it when the address is not yet on the
# contract). Renaming afterwards leaves your reports saying one name while the
# chain says another.
#
# So an operator who copies this file unchanged runs a verifier identical to
# one already in the market. That is worse than it sounds. The whole reason a
# task asks several verifiers is that they are several opinions; two running
# the same model are one opinion counted twice, and their agreement means
# nothing while still costing the task a full share of the bounty.
#
# Be careful about what the other two numbers buy, because the preset table
# above suggests more than the code delivers:
#
#   SUPPORT_THRESHOLD is read only by the offline lexical scorer, which runs
#   when a compute call fails. A verifier whose model answers never consults
#   it, and an offline verdict is excluded from consensus anyway. A threshold
#   nobody else uses changes nothing about the verdicts you publish.
#
#   EVIDENCE_DEPTH sets how many candidate spans the model is shown — floored
#   at three — and how many of its citations are kept. Candidates are the best
#   span from each source, so the count is capped by how many sources the task
#   named: on a one- or two-source task, depth 2, 3 and 4 show the model
#   exactly the same spans. Depth changes what the model sees only on a task
#   with four or more sources.
#
# Both are published in your onchain metadata, so two verifiers can look
# different in the directory while being the same verifier. Independence comes
# from the model.
#
# So: pick a COMPUTE_MODEL from a different vendor family than the live
# verifiers run — the directory on the web app lists what each one runs. Pin
# depth and threshold explicitly too if you want them held against a change to
# the presets, but do not mistake them for a second opinion.
#
# VERIFIER_A_EVIDENCE_DEPTH=3
# VERIFIER_A_SUPPORT_THRESHOLD=0.62

# Rewards are pull-based and collecting costs gas. Hold until the unclaimed
# total covers it (default 0.002 0G); 0 collects after every task.
VERIFIER_MIN_COLLECT_WEI=2000000000000000

# ─── 0G Storage ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Your revealed reports are written here on your own key. Leave
# STORAGE_PRIVATE_KEY blank to pay from the verifier key above; set it only to
# separate the fee-paying key from the signing one.
STORAGE_DRIVER=zerog
STORAGE_INDEXER_RPC=
STORAGE_PRIVATE_KEY=
STORAGE_ROOT=.proofrelay/storage

# ─── 0G Compute ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# zerog-router: a key from https://pc.0g.ai. The model defaults to one every
#   mainnet provider can seed (deepseek-v4-flash); this pipeline sends a seed
#   on every completion and refuses a provider that would drop it.
# zerog-broker: no account. COMPUTE_PRIVATE_KEY funds an onchain ledger and
#   signs each request; COMPUTE_MODEL is then required and must be an id from
#   the onchain registry, which is a different catalog from the router's.
# local: the deterministic offline engine — for trying the loop, not for
#   earning. Reports say which engine produced them.
#
# CHOOSING A MODEL, on the router. The default is what verifiers already in the
# market run, so setting your own is the other half of not being a duplicate.
# Read GET https://router-api.0g.ai/v1/providers (public, no key) and keep only
# models that satisfy all four:
#   1. the id also appears in GET /v1/models — that is the route the driver
#      probes at startup, and not every id in /v1/providers is routable under
#      the name it uses there
#   2. supported_parameters contains "seed" — a seed is sent on every call and
#      X-0G-Provider-Require-Parameters makes the router refuse a provider that
#      would drop it, so an unseedable model fails rather than drifting
#   3. tee_acknowledged is true, because COMPUTE_VERIFY_TEE below is
#   4. supported_parameters also has temperature, top_p, max_tokens and
#      response_format — every one of them is sent
# Then check the verdict wording before you trust it. The driver accepts only
# SUPPORTED, CONTRADICTED and INSUFFICIENT_EVIDENCE; a model that writes
# CONTRADICT is refused per claim and that claim silently falls to the offline
# scorer. `npm run check:compute` reports exactly that as PARTIAL.
COMPUTE_DRIVER=zerog-router
COMPUTE_API_KEY=
COMPUTE_MODEL=
COMPUTE_PRIVATE_KEY=
# 90s suits a flash model. A slower one needs more: hy4-preview measured 85.2s
# on a two-claim task where deepseek-v4-flash took 5.8s, which left under five
# seconds of headroom and, on a busier run, none. Two things bite when a model
# is slow, and only the first is this setting. The second is the task's commit
# window — 120s by default — which has to hold your inference plus a storage
# upload plus two transactions. Measure yours with `npm run check:compute`; the
# OK line prints the milliseconds.
# Bounds reasoning AND answer together. A model that thinks before it writes
# can spend the whole budget thinking and return empty content — which is what
# hy4-preview did on mainnet task 0x142f4f1b…, having used 398 of 455 completion
# tokens on reasoning for one trivial claim. The driver refuses an empty answer
# and the claim falls to the offline scorer, which earns nothing. If your model
# has `reasoning_content` in its replies, raise this to 8000 or more.
COMPUTE_MAX_TOKENS=2048

COMPUTE_TIMEOUT_MS=90000
COMPUTE_VERIFY_TEE=true
COMPUTE_TRUST_MODE=verified
COMPUTE_REQUIRE_PARAMETERS=true
COMPUTE_SEED=1337
